<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ardagh, County Longford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ardagh is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-longford">County Longford</a>, Ireland, whose church is traditionally said to have been founded by Saint Mel, a disciple of Saint Patrick, in the 5th century. It became the seat of the medieval diocese of Ardagh, and the ruins of a small early stone church survive there. The Catholic diocese of Ardagh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a> still bears its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardagh-county-longford.jpg" alt="Ardagh, County Longford" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurel Lodged · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Patrick%27s_church,_Ardagh,_County_Longford.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ardagh, from the Irish Ardachadh, high field, lies in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-longford">County Longford</a>, 10 kilometres southwest of Longford town, beside Ardagh Mountain, a hill of 650 feet. The hill, formerly Brí Leith, was held to be the home of Midir, a pre-Christian king of the Tuatha De Danann, and the Book of Rights records that the high king was entitled to bilberries from it for his harvest meal. The site was a place of pre-Christian worship before Christian settlement began, and Saint Mel, its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and patron, is thought to be buried there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement, church and houses were burned in 1167, and in 1230 a contest for the bishopric destroyed the episcopal house and the cathedral tower. William O'Ferrall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> from 1479, tried to make himself chieftain of the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaly">Annaly</a> in place of another branch of the O'Ferrall family, and the attack on Ardagh that followed left the cathedral without roof, sacristy, campanile or bell. The village grew little until the Featherston family arrived; Thomas Featherston acquired 235 acres in 1703.</p><p>Local legend holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-goldsmith">Oliver Goldsmith</a>, sent in jest to the Featherston house when he asked for an inn, drew on the mistake for She Stoops to Conquer. In the 1860s the Fetherston baronets had much of the village laid out by the architect J. Rawson Carroll, reputedly after Lady Fetherston's idea of a Swiss village, with a clock tower, a green and arts-and-crafts houses of ashlar limestone or sandstone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The village green is still its centre, with roads to Longford, Carrickboy and Edgeworthstown, and a Heritage Centre tells its history. Ardagh won the Irish Tidy Towns Competition in 1989, 1996 and 1998, and gives its name to a barony and a civil parish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ardagh, County Longford” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monasteries such as <b>Ardagh</b> and Clonmacnoise's daughter houses stood in or near its lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tethba">Kingdom of Tethba</a></li></ul></section>
